</image>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-polar-volume-tikz">
<caption>
The solid of <xref ref="example-polar-volume"/>: the region under the
paraboloid <m>z = x^2 + y^2</m>, above the <m>xy</m>-plane, and inside
the cylinder <m>x^2 + y^2 = 2x</m>. Its volume is
<m>\frac{3\pi}{2}</m>.
</caption>
<image width="70%">
<description>
Three coordinate axes in space with a vertical circular cylinder of
radius one centered on the line x equals one, y equals zero. Inside
the cylinder, the paraboloid z equals x squared plus y squared rises
from the origin; the solid between the x y plane and the paraboloid,
inside the cylinder, is the region whose volume is computed. Dashed
lines mark the base circle.
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[line join=round, line cap=round]
  % Okabe-Ito colorblind-safe palette
  \definecolor{oiblue}{RGB}{0,114,178}
  \definecolor{oivermillion}{RGB}{213,94,0}
  \begin{axis}[
      view={125}{22},
      width=12.5cm,
      axis lines=center,
      axis line style={-latex, black!75, line width=0.7pt},
      xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
      label style={font=\large},
      ticks=none,
      xmin=0, xmax=2.7, ymin=-1.5, ymax=1.6, zmin=0, zmax=4.9,
      clip=false,
    ]
    % ---- base disk boundary r = 2 cos(theta) ----
    \addplot3[oivermillion, line width=1.4pt, domain=0:360, samples=97,
        samples y=0, forget plot]
      ({1 + cos(x)}, {sin(x)}, 0);
    % ---- cylinder wall ----
    \addplot3[surf, shader=interp,
              colormap={softc}{color=(oiblue!8!white) color=(oiblue!30!white)},
              opacity=0.30, fill opacity=0.30,
              domain=0:360, samples=49,
              y domain=0:1, samples y=2,
              z buffer=sort, forget plot]
      ({1 + cos(x)}, {sin(x)}, {y*4.4});
    % ---- paraboloid z = r^2 over the disk: parametrize by s (angle), t ----
    % point (1 + t cos s, t sin s), z = (1+t cos s)^2 + (t sin s)^2
    \addplot3[surf, shader=faceted, faceted color=black!45,
              point meta={z},
              colormap={softz}{color=(oiblue!15!white) color=(oiblue!50!white)},
              opacity=0.65, fill opacity=0.65,
              domain=0:360, samples=37,
              y domain=0:1, samples y=13,
              z buffer=sort, forget plot]
      ({1 + y*cos(x)}, {y*sin(x)},
       {(1 + y*cos(x))^2 + (y*sin(x))^2});
    % ---- rim: intersection of paraboloid and cylinder ----
    \addplot3[oiblue, line width=1.4pt, domain=0:360, samples=97,
        samples y=0, forget plot]
      ({1 + cos(x)}, {sin(x)}, {(1 + cos(x))^2 + (sin(x))^2});
    % ---- labels ----
    \node[font=\large, anchor=north west] at (axis description cs:0.0,0.98)
      {$z = x^2 + y^2$};
    \node[oivermillion, font=\small, anchor=north]
      at (axis cs:1.0,-1.35,-0.25) {$x^2 + y^2 = 2x$};
  \end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-polar-gaussian-example">
<title>An Integral That Needs Polar Coordinates</title>

<example xml:id="example-polar-gaussian">
<title>Example IV</title>
<statement>
<p>
Calculate the double integral
<m>\displaystyle\iint_R e^{x^2+y^2}\,dx\,dy</m>, where <m>R</m> is the
unit disk centered at the origin.
</p>

<p>
<em>Hint:</em> Notice that you cannot solve this integral using
Cartesian coordinates.
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<solution>
<p>
In polar coordinates the unit disk is
<m>0 \le r \le 1</m>, <m>0 \le \theta \le 2\pi</m>, and
<m>e^{x^2+y^2} = e^{r^2}</m>. The extra factor of <m>r</m> in
<m>dA = r\,dr\,d\theta</m> is exactly what makes the integral
computable, via the substitution <m>u = r^2</m>:
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
\iint_R e^{x^2+y^2}\,dx\,dy
\amp= \int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^1 e^{r^2}\,r\,dr\,d\theta
= \int_0^{2\pi}\left[\tfrac12 e^{r^2}\right]_0^1 d\theta
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= \int_0^{2\pi}\tfrac12(e - 1)\,d\theta
= \pi(e-1).
</mrow>
</md>
</solution>

</subsection>

</section>
